        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:06:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62009,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Patients typically present with abnormal uterine bleeding or symptoms related to an ovarian mass, but some tumours are incidental findings; the large majority of cases are unilateral. Hormonal symptoms are uncommon, but virilization and precocious puberty have been reported. Rare tumours are associated with Meigs syndrome {15285324; 20925634}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T17:56:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62010,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are uncommon. Sclerosing stromal tumours generally occur in young women and girls, with a mean patient age of 29&nbsp;years in small clinicopathological series {4348335; 20135135; 27149006; 28982854}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:06:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62011,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:06:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62012,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "<p>FISH studies on a small subset of tumours have revealed a subpopulation of tumour cells with trisomy&nbsp;12 {9422320; 15032299; 17990931}. Recently, recurrent <em>FHL2</em>-<em>GLI2</em> fusion genes have been demonstrated in 17&nbsp;of 26&nbsp;sclerosing stromal tumours (65%) and other <em>GLI2</em> rearrangements in an additional 15% of cases (4&nbsp;of 26) {31896750}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T17:56:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62013,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours range from 1.5 to 19&nbsp;cm (mean: 11&nbsp;cm) {4348335; 20135135; 27149006; 28982854}. They are well circumscribed and usually show a solid yellow to white cut surface. Central oedema and cyst formation may be seen.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:06:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62014,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Sclerosing stromal tumour has a pseudolobular appearance, with cellular nodules separated by a hypocellular oedematous, collagenous, or occasionally myxoid stroma. Thin-walled dilated vessels, often with a haemangiopericytoma-like appearance, are typically present. Nodules are composed of an admixture of bland epithelioid and spindled cells. The former have clear to eosinophilic vacuolated cytoplasm, sometimes resulting in a signet-ring appearance, and may show prominent luteinization, especially during pregnancy {4348335; 25851706}. Mitotic activity is low, but significant numbers of mitoses are seen in a small subset of cases {27149006}. Tumours are usually positive for sex cord markers, such as inhibin and calretinin, but negative for cytokeratins and EMA. TFE3 expression has been reported in a subset of tumours {28982854}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:06:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62015,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 71,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:06:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
